Intentionally NOT vendored:
  Source/Templates/system_stm32g0xx.c
      The SystemInit() implementation, and the only .c file this component would
      contribute.  It is NOT needed and is deliberately absent: the NeurOne
      startup code does not call SystemInit.  Reset_Handler in
      firmware/safety_mcu/startup/startup_stm32g071xx.s copies .data, zeroes .bss
      and branches straight to main — verified by reading the assembly, which is
      the reason this was determined rather than assumed.  (The file's own header
      comment says "then call SystemInit and main"; the comment is wrong and the
      code is the record.  Flagged as OI-SWCI-19.)  Nothing in SW-01 references
      SystemInit, SystemCoreClock, AHBPrescTable or APBPrescTable — those are
      declarations only in system_stm32g0xx.h, so no definition is required to
      link.  Clock bring-up is np_hal_clock_init(), first-party code.
  Source/Templates/gcc/startup_stm32g071xx.s and the IAR/KEIL equivalents
      NeurOne has its own startup and vector table (above), which is first-party
      Class C code and not SOUP.
  Source/Templates/gcc/linker/*.ld
      NeurOne uses firmware/safety_mcu/startup/stm32g071_flash.ld.
  Include/stm32g0*.h for every other STM32G0 part
      (g030/g031/g041/g050/g051/g061/g070/g081/g0b0/g0b1/g0c1).  SW-01 ships on
      STM32G071 only (NP-SW-001 §5.1, CLAUDE.md §4.1).

  THE ST HAL AND LL DRIVERS ARE NOT VENDORED — and are not part of this component
  at all.  This resolves OI-SWCI-06 in favour of device headers only.  They live
  in a separate upstream repository (STMicroelectronics/stm32g0xx-hal-driver);
  bringing them in would add tens of thousands of lines of substantive third-party
  *logic* to Class C SOUP surface, each line carrying the §7.1.2 obligation, in
  exchange for nothing.  The safety MCU makes ZERO HAL_* and ZERO LL_* calls —
  measured with word-boundary matching (`rg '\bHAL_|\bLL_[A-Z]'` over
  firmware/safety_mcu/{src,include,startup}: no matches).  Note that a naive
  substring search for "LL_" appears to find hits; they are NP_SAFETY_EN_ALL_MASK
  and NP_THERMAL_POLL_MS, and they are not driver calls.  The firmware is
  register-level throughout.

  Consequently USE_HAL_DRIVER is NOT defined by the build.  stm32g0xx.h ends with
      #if defined (USE_HAL_DRIVER)
       #include "stm32g0xx_hal.h"
      #endif
  so defining it would demand the un-vendored HAL and hard-fail the compile.  It
  *was* defined in firmware/safety_mcu/CMakeLists.txt before this change, where it
  had never been load-bearing because no device header existed in-tree to act on
  it; removing it is part of this vendoring.  A regression test for exactly this
  is recorded as mutant M2 in NP-SW-CI-001 §6.5.
